    "featured_image": "/assets/products/brochures.jpg",
    "image_alt": "Technical pre-press artwork preparation showing bleed and trim marks",
    "meta_title": "How to Prepare Print-Ready PDF Artwork: Technical Prepress Guide | ONPRINT",
    "meta_description": "Essential prepress guide for designers in Dubai. Master CMYK color conversion, 3mm bleed, safe margins, font outlining, and 300 DPI resolution.",
    "focus_keyword": "print ready artwork dubai",
    "secondary_keywords": "bleed and trim marks printing, cmyk color mode prepress, 300 dpi resolution dubai, preflight pdf checklist",
    "canonical_url": "https://www.0nprint.com/blog/how-to-prepare-print-ready-pdf-artwork",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Why does my printed color look darker than my computer screen?",
        "answer": "Computer monitors are backlit with glowing light, whereas printed paper reflects ambient light. Furthermore, RGB has a broader color gamut than CMYK ink, which can cause slight dulling if colors are not properly calibrated in prepress software.",
        "is_approved": true
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I submit a Canva file for commercial printing?",
        "answer": "Yes, provided you export from Canva using \"PDF Print\" format, select CMYK color profile, and check the box for \"Crop marks and bleed\".",
        "is_approved": true
      }
    ],
    "content": "\n      <h2>The Bridge Between Screen Design and Physical Print</h2>\n      <p>One of the most frequent friction points in commercial printing occurs when beautiful digital designs prepared in Adobe Illustrator, InDesign, or Photoshop fail to translate accurately onto physical paper. Text shifts, backgrounds reveal white borders, and vibrant digital colors turn dull or muddy.</p>\n\n      <h2>The Four Pillars of Print-Ready Artwork</h2>\n      <h3>1. Color Space: CMYK vs. RGB</h3>\n      <p>Computer screens emit light using the additive RGB color spectrum (Red, Green, Blue). Commercial printing presses deposit subtractive cyan, magenta, yellow, and black inks (CMYK) onto light-reflecting paper. Designing in RGB leads to unexpected color shifts, particularly with bright greens, neon blues, and intense oranges. Always convert artwork to <strong>FOGRA39 / ISO Coated v2 (CMYK)</strong> before export.</p>\n\n      <h3>2. Resolution: The 300 DPI Rule</h3>\n      <p>Web graphics typically display at 72 or 96 DPI (Dots Per Inch). When printed at commercial resolution, low-DPI images appear pixelated and fuzzy. Ensure all embedded photographs, logos, and raster assets maintain a minimum effective resolution of <strong>300 DPI at 100% printed scale</strong>.</p>\n\n      <h3>3. Bleed and Safe Zones</h3>\n      <ul>\n        <li><strong>Bleed (3mm):</strong> Background images, colors, and design elements that extend to the outer edge of the page must continue 3mm beyond the final trim line. This accommodates microscopic blade tolerances during guillotine cutting.</li>\n        <li><strong>Trim Line:</strong> The exact finished boundary of your printed piece.</li>\n        <li><strong>Safe Margin (4mm):</strong> Critical text, phone numbers, and logos must sit at least 4mm inside the trim line to ensure they are never clipped by the cutter blade.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h3>4. Typography: Outlining All Fonts</h3>\n      <p>If a commercial press RIP (Raster Image Processor) does not have your exact custom typography installed, it will substitute standard system fonts, altering your kerning and layout. Always select all text in Adobe Illustrator and press <code>Ctrl+Shift+O</code> (Cmd+Shift+O on Mac) to convert fonts into static vector outlines.</p>\n\n      <h2>The Pre-Export Preflight Checklist</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>All colors verified in CMYK (or specified Pantone spot colors).</li>\n        <li>Images verified at 300 DPI minimum.</li>\n        <li>3mm external bleed included on all sides.</li>\n        <li>All text converted to vector outlines.</li>\n        <li>PDF exported using PDF/X-1a:2001 or High Quality Print standard.</li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <p>Submit your verified artwork to our <a href=\"/get-a-quote\">Free Pre-Press Artwork Audit Tool</a>.</p>\n    "
